  "live_records": {
    "q-143690": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143690",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0$ at $\\theta =\\pi ,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\pi $\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*",
      "worked_solution": "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\pi )=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\pi $ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the limit in part II equals $0,$ there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/d1308067e18807f159aee95433ea645be71abd153aa03888ef8092cf6fe226bd.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
