      "knowledge_point": "Finding Horizontal Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases",
      "kp_id": "e-10116"
    },
    "q-143720": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143720",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*",
      "worked_solution": "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/8b68110c25bfa464c0971a396ec50908c0c0622cbdc92bf42239b984695655ca.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
