    "q-143555": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143555",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\pi /2$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*",
      "worked_solution": "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0-0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-2\\cdot (-1)}{0+2\\cdot 0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/2c1b42bf3a8c1b5f8755e02a162e1554904bbc432285cb8ba8c7b06181e1cc03.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
