    "q-143586": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143586",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3$ for $\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0$ at $\\theta =0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*",
      "worked_solution": "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ))^{′}}{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{-3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/9ef9840644e37fbb660ede7d574d8814b9ce7ce3bd0ff3790b5c5d21420f3e70.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
