    "q-143691": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143691",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0$ at $\\theta =0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*",
      "worked_solution": "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{sin}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)(3{cos}^{2}⁡(0)-{sin}^{2}⁡(0))}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (3\\cdot 1-0)}{2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the limit in part II equals $0,$ there curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/a48593a3bbcc61afaf80de619be505cb6dac58c559e03a0eab4553bd5a1e5013.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
