    "q-145010": {
      "dom_id": "step-q145010",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{-} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{-}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))^{′}}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi ) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2\\cdot {0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, and the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2\\cdot {0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/f9a4fc8fa61dac84d3dbc4bc48f86e2b702f7854b1fd1d3b3e5f4303fe7769a7.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
