    "q-145211": {
      "dom_id": "step-q145211",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r=8{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta -8{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$",
      "worked_solution": "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {0}^{-},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{24{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{16{sin}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{24\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)(3{cos}^{2}⁡(0)-1)}{24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{24} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the right limit of our derivative is not infinite, the curve does *not* have a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0.$ In fact, it is a horizontal tangent.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/4a525b9a7a04f228bb45dc8c0c1cb916d1e98d8907e5fb1411ea52eb964c96ef.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
