    "q-145215": {
      "dom_id": "step-q145215",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2),\\,\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$",
      "worked_solution": "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & ={tan}^{2}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)({cos}^{2}⁡(0)+2) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1\\cdot ({1}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {0}^{+},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2)}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{{tan}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)({cos}^{2}⁡(0)+2)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 1\\cdot ({1}^{2}+2)}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the right limit of our derivative is not infinite, the curve does *not* have a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0.$ In fact, it is a horizontal tangent.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/498b937eba18870a36ee6f09bb99cf7dde38eb95c4cc2aa6edcb46080d0fcf04.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
